# tiko-rt was developed as its own repository with an all-LF working tree.
# Bringing it in here, under core.autocrlf=true, rewrote all 364 files to CRLF on
# checkout. That is harmless for the .bas sources but not for the rest: build/*.sh
# is executed, and tests/abi/baseline/*.summary is diffed against freshly generated
# output that has LF. Pin the whole subtree back to what it was built and gated as.
#
# Scoped to lib/rt on purpose -- the compiler's own tree has a mix already, and
# renormalising it is not part of this move.
lib/rt/** text eol=lf

# THE COMPILER'S OWN SHELL SCRIPTS, for the same reason and with the same fix.
# They were a mix: build/build.sh and build/test.sh happened to be LF, while
# build/lint.sh and build/mirsweep.sh were CRLF, so under WSL lint.sh died on
#
#     build/lint.sh: line 29: exit: 2: numeric argument required
#
# -- the '\r' is part of the argument. Worse, a FRESH checkout on Windows gets
# CRLF for ALL of them plus build/sources.txt, so 'git worktree add' produced a
# tree whose Linux build could not start at all: 'syntax error near unexpected
# token $'in\r'' from build.sh, then error 81 from fbc on "src/driver/main.tiko\r".
#
# sources.txt is here because it is READ BY a script -- grep hands each line to
# fbc as an argument -- not because it is one.
#
# tests/vendor/ is fbc's own suite and has shell scripts of its own; it is not
# named here and is not renormalised, deliberately.
build/*.sh text eol=lf
build/sources.txt text eol=lf
lib/gfx/tools/*.sh text eol=lf
tools/*.sh text eol=lf
tools/gen/*.sh text eol=lf

# TWO DERIVED-TRUTH FILES, for the same reason lib/rt's baselines are pinned:
# they are generated with LF by tools/gen/{layout,mangle}-truth.sh and then read
# back BY A SHELL SCRIPT, which is byte-exact.
#
#   check-mangle.sh  diff -u "$want" ...          a CR is a differing character
#   check-layout.sh  while read -r name size align   the CR lands in $align, so
#                                                 EVERY builtin type mismatches
#
# Their twelve siblings in tests/types/expected/fbc-*.txt are NOT pinned and do
# not need to be: those are read by tools/gen/check-*.py through
# read_text().splitlines(), which is universal-newline and drops the CR. Two of
# them sit CRLF in the working tree today and nothing notices.
tests/mangle/expected/fbc-mangle.txt text eol=lf
tests/types/expected/fbc-layout.txt  text eol=lf
tests/types/expected/fbc-layout-linux-x86_64.txt text eol=lf

# The same hazard, in lib/gfx: golden/*.txt is a RECORDING, compared byte for
# byte against output freshly emitted with LF line endings, and build/*.sh and
# tools/*.sh are executed. Under core.autocrlf=true a checkout would rewrite
# both, and the abtest gate would report every recording STALE on a fresh clone.
lib/gfx/tools/golden/** text eol=lf
lib/gfx/**/*.sh text eol=lf

# THE SUITE IS BYTES, NOT TEXT. Goldens, expected outputs and sources are compared byte
# for byte, so a checkout under core.autocrlf=true -- the Windows default -- rewrote
# them to CRLF and 43 tests failed on a fresh clone (audit F-66). -text stores and
# checks out exactly what was committed.
tests/** -text

# lib/implib: the windows-x86_64 import-library sources (build/implibs.sh). The .def files are
# text and LF like the rest of the tree; the static GUID archives are binary.
lib/implib/*.def text eol=lf
lib/implib/static/*.lib binary

# #embed fixtures are bytes: never convert their line endings.
tests/**/embed/** -text
tests/**/embedmod/** -text

# Zone files copied unchanged from a tzdata release: binary, never converted.
tests/codegen/tzdata/** binary
tests/codegen/tzdata-slim/** binary
pkg/std/tzdata/*.tzdb binary
